BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 677.0 Tm (The original Amiga file system appeared with the first Commodore Amiga machines in 1985.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 660.0 Tm (The DOS0 format suited the floppy-centred Amiga environment and its operating system's) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 643.0 Tm (distinctive handler and device model. The Fast File System later changed file-data layout) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 626.0 Tm (for better throughput on hard disks; it was not merely an optimised implementation of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 609.0 Tm (identical OFS data blocks.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 580.0 Tm (Amiga filesystem modes carry DOS type identifiers: original DOS0, international DOS2,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 563.0 Tm (directory-cache DOS4 and later long-name variants. The AmigaOS documentation identifies the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 546.0 Tm (international change with correct handling of accented filename case equivalence, while) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 529.0 Tm (directory caching was introduced to accelerate floppy directory listing. These variants are) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 512.0 Tm (historical format decisions that require explicit handling, not silent assumptions about) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 495.0 Tm (every Amiga disk.) Tj ET
